// Dam break: the gate of a reservoir is snatched up out of the water, the way
// the gate of a laboratory dam break is, and the whole reservoir collapses
// down the valley as one body of water.
//
// The reservoir is 149,040 cells of water (41 m long and 36 m deep, at 10
// cells per metre) held by a steel gate 2.4 m thick standing on a concrete
// sill, hung from the housing of its hoist. At tick 40 the hoist's brake lets
// go, the counterweights fling the gate up into its housing in a quarter of a
// second, and nothing is left in the water's way. Nothing is dug and no water
// is removed.
//
// The face of the reservoir then collapses as the shallow water equations say
// it should: the surface slumps into the smooth sloping profile of a dam
// break while the far end of the lake still lies flat, and the front runs out
// over the apron, down the slope and across the dry valley at about 25 m/s,
// hugging the ground. It slams into the far hillside and runs some 25 m up it,
// throwing spray off its tip, falls back as a bore that travels upstream to
// the apron, and the new lake sloshes from end to end for the rest of the
// run. The [expect] lines count every cell of water at the start and at the
// end.
//
// Nothing stands in the valley. Bodies and buildings in the path of the flood
// are not pushed by it (bodies feel buoyancy and still-water drag only), and
// the water piles up against them in vertical blocks, so the flood has the
// valley to itself.
[scene]
name = dam break
description = A steel floodgate is flung up out of a 36 m deep reservoir: the whole reservoir collapses through the gap as one body, races down the valley at 25 m/s, runs 25 m up the far hillside and falls back as a bore.
size = 2048 1152
seed = 22
ticks = 1200

[build]
// Bedrock floor and walls.
fill 0 1132 2048 1152 bedrock
fill 0 0 8 1132 bedrock
fill 2040 0 2048 1132 bedrock
// The ground: stone under a 3.6 m soil skin, one run of columns per line.
// A cliff closes the far end of the reservoir; the apron runs out from the
// gate; the valley floor lies 10 m lower and a hillside closes it.
